\title{Talagrand's Convex Distance Inequality on Finite Product Spaces:\\
A Complete Development with the Optimal Constant}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{20 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
We give a complete, self-contained development of Talagrand's convex distance
inequality on finite product spaces, with the classical optimal constant $1/4$
in the exponent, for arbitrary product measures with independent---but not
necessarily identically distributed---coordinates. The central result is the
exponential moment bound $\EE[e^{\dT(A,X)^2/4}]\PP(A)\le 1$, where $\dT(A,x)$ is
the Euclidean distance from the origin to the convex hull of the disagreement
vectors $(\one{x_i\neq y_i})_{i\le n}$, $y\in A$. From it we derive the
deviation inequality $\PP(A)\PP(S)\le e^{-t/4}$ whenever $\dT^2(A,\cdot)\ge t$ on
$S$, the weighted Hamming form $\PP(A)\PP(S)\le e^{-t^2/4}$, concentration of
$1$-Lipschitz functionals about a median with constant $2$, and the
isoperimetric statement $\PP(A)\ge 1/2 \Rightarrow \PP(\dT^2\ge t)\le 2e^{-t/4}$.
We further establish a minimax (duality) identity expressing $\dT$ as a supremum
of weighted Hamming distances, exact evaluations of the convex distance to a
singleton and to a subcube, concentration of certifiable functionals at the
certificate scale, and two applications---counting ones among arbitrarily biased
independent coins, and the longest increasing subsequence of a random
word---where that scale strictly improves on bounded differences. A separate
analytic layer isolates a weighted H\"older inequality and an interpolation
lemma, $e^{(1-\lambda)^2/4}r^{-\lambda}\le 2-r$, whose optimal parameter is
$\lambda = 1+2\log r$ and which reduces to the scalar inequality
$e^{u-u^2}\le 2-e^{-u}$ on $[0,\tfrac12]$; this third-order tangency at $u=0$
pinpoints the optimality of the constant $1/4$.
\end{abstract}

